UID:b83e015085da84ec@role-professional-responsibility.maine-government-fee
 ds
DTSTAMP:20260923T165336Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260923
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] 9/23/2026: Reminders in Preparation for Maine
  eCourts and eFiling in Cumberland County Courts
DESCRIPTION:Home\nSkip to top navigation\nSkip to side navigation\nSkip to
  content\nSkip to footer\nMaine.gov\nAgencies\n|\nOnline Services\n|\nHelp
 \n|\nSearch Maine.gov\nCOURT ALERTS IN EFFECT.\nSee Active Alerts\nHome\n
 →\nNews & Announcements\nReminders in Preparation for Maine eCourts and 
 eFiling in Cumberland County Courts\nDate:\n9/23/2026\nOn Monday\, Septemb
 er 28\, 2026\, the Maine Judicial Branch is expanding Maine eCourts to all
  case types in Judicial Region 2\, comprising Cumberland County Unified Cr
 iminal Docket (UCD)\, Cumberland County Superior Court\, Portland District
  Court\, and Bridgton District Court.\nAll case types -- civil\, family\, 
 juvenile and criminal cases -- will be included in the transition from pap
 er filing to electronic filing. As of Monday\, September 28\, all mandator
 y electronic filers pursuant to the\nMaine Rules of Electronic Court Syste
 ms (MRECS)\nwill be required to eFile documents in these courts. Self-repr
 esented litigants (parties who do not have attorneys) who file fewer than 
 six cases in a calendar year will not be required to eFile.\nCourt Closure
 s and Emergency Filing Procedures\nTo prepare for the implementation of Ma
 ine eCourts and eFiling on September 28\, Region 2 courts are currently op
 erating on a modified schedule and have emergency filing procedures in pla
 ce. In addition\, all Region 2 courts will be closed from 8 a.m. to noon t
 he week of September 28th. Please refer to the\nschedule of closures and e
 mergency procedures during these closings\n.\nShareFile Discontinued in Re
 gion 2 Courts\nThe ShareFile platform used in Region 2 courts will be disa
 bled for all criminal\, civil violation\, juvenile filings\, and GAL vouch
 ers\, at 2:00 p.m. on Thursday\, September 24. Starting Monday\, September
  28\, attorneys must instead use\neFileMaine\n. Please note\, attorney vou
 chers filed pursuant to the\nStanding Order regarding Payment to Court-App
 ointed Counsel\nwill continue to be accepted by ShareFile for a grace peri
 od of 60 days.\nTraining Resources\nFor attorneys and staff who are new to
 \nhttps://www.courts.maine.gov/news/article.html?id=13362444
URL:https://www.courts.maine.gov/news/article.html?id=13362444
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:82db1537c0d03a5a@role-professional-responsibility.maine-government-fee
 ds
DTSTAMP:20260921T215842Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260625
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Wolford v. Lopez
DESCRIPTION:PRELIMINARY PRINT \nVolume 609 U. S. Part 1 \nPages 185–257 
 \nOFFICIAL REPORTS \nOF \nTHE SUPREME COURT \nJune 25\, 2026 \nREBECCA A. 
 WOMELDORF \nreporter of decisions \nPage Proof Pending Publication\nNOTICE
 : This preliminary print is subject to formal revision before \nthe bound 
 volume is published. Users are requested to notify the Reporter \nof Decis
 ions\, Supreme Court of the United States\, Washington\, D. C. 20543\, \np
 io@supremecourt.gov\, of any typographical or other formal errors. \nPage 
 Proof Pending Publication\nOCTOBER TERM\, 2025 185 \nSyllabus \nWOLFORD et
  al. v. LOPEZ\, ATTORNEY GENERAL \nOF HAWAII \ncertiorari to the united st
 ates court of appeals for \nthe ninth circuit \nNo. 24–1046. Argued Janu
 ary 20\, 2026—Decided June 25\, 2026 \nFor years\, the State of Hawaii m
 ade it almost impossible to obtain a li-\ncense to carry a frearm. Four ye
 ars ago\, however\, this Court held in \nNew York State Rife & Pistol Assn
 .\, Inc. v. Bruen\, 597 U. S. 1\, that the \nSecond and Fourteenth Amendme
 nts protect the right to carry hand-\nguns outside the home for self-defen
 se. Hawaii responded by replacing \nits old law on carry permits with new 
 laws that achieved a similar result. \nAt issue in this case is a Hawaii l
 aw that prohibits frearms on private \nproperty open to the public without
  the express and affrmative consent \nof the property owner. Hawaii's new 
 rule imposes severe restrictions \non the daily activities of residents wh
 o have satisfed the State's rigor-\nous requirements for the issuance of a
  carry permit. When these per-\nmit holders leave home\, not only must the
 y take care to avoid all the \nterritory where the possession of a gun is 
 prohibited outright\, but they \nmay also be barred from entering many pla
 ces that people routinely \nvisit in the course of their daily routines\, 
 such as gas stations\, restau-\nrants\, and stores. This law fips the defa
 ult rule at common law\, under \nwhich anyone has an implied license to en
 ter property held open to the \npublic unless the property owner withdraws
  consen\nhttps://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/609us1r58_a8cf.pdf
URL:https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/609us1r58_a8cf.pdf
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
